00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:36.910
There are estimated to be around 6,500 spoken
languages in the world today.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:42.510
But more than half the world’s population
uses only 20 of them and a language dies out

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.239
every 14 days.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:50.879
The most spoken language is Mandarin, with
955 million native speakers, followed by Spanish

00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:55.560
with 470 million and then English with 360
million.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.109
But how did language originate?

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:00.629
And why don’t we all speak the same language?

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:09.090
Let’s find out.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:14.770
Homo sapiens have been on earth for around
7 million years, and modern humans as we know

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.100
them today have been around for 200,000 years.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.630
The human race is ancient, but we’ve only
been able to communicate with each other via

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.280
language for a, rather short, 100,000 years.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.100
Scientists believe that humans have always
possessed the building blocks for language,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:39.700
but it wasn’t until 100,000 years ago when
a chance mutation occurred in a single human

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.470
that put the final building block in place
inside the brain, which enabled humans to

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.380
finally be able to talk using legible words.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:53.930
It’s thought that the very first words were
emulations of noises that would have been

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:59.630
heard in nature, such as the noises of animals,
and the wind blowing through the trees.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:04.660
Scientists theorise that the very first words
spoken by humans could very well have sounded

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:10.360
like these: Bow-wow, Pooh-pooh, Ding-dong,
Yo-he-ho and Ta-ta.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:17.190
But the truth is we know very little about
primitive languages because writing wasn’t

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:23.550
developed until much later, and without writing
we have almost no evidence of ancient languages.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.530
The first written language we have found evidence
for is ancient Egyptian.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:33.270
We have found Egyptian hieroglyphs dating
back as far as 2690 BC.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:40.850
But why do we have different languages, wouldn’t
it be much simpler if we all spoke the same

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:41.850
language?

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.870
To understand this we first need to understand
how languages develop.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:53.930
thousands of years ago, isolated civilisations
across the globe all had the same problem,

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:58.780
they needed a way to describe the things they
owned, the tools they used and the things

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.790
they did, to their fellow citizens.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:06.970
So they came up with words for all their tools,
possessions and actions, these names would

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.850
start with one person calling an object by
a made up word, and after referring to it

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.510
as that word for some time.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:19.380
Other people would begin calling it by the
same name, and eventually, the word would

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.380
stick.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.960
This enabled them to work together more efficiently
and get more done.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.840
Today we can get from one side of the planet
to the other in just a few hours, but back

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.680
then travel took so long that most people
didn’t bother.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.241
This meant most groups of people where in
isolation from the rest of the world, this

00:03:38.241 --> 00:03:42.990
led to thousands of different groups of people
coming up with thousands of different words

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.290
for various objects.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.240
That’s why some people may call this an
apple, and other would call it a pomme.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.040
Over time these isolated groups of people
met with other isolated groups of people,

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.360
when this happened they needed a common way
to describe objects, so they both adapted

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:05.069
their self taught languages to create a new
language that was a mix of the both.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:10.040
This happened time and time again, and after
enough groups of people mixed their languages

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:17.880
together, a new super-language was created,
which was a combination of all the sub groups.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.310
This would eventually grow to become one of
the many languages we know today, such as

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.460
English or French.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:33.100
But proximity has a huge effect on how languages
develop, countries next to each other often

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:42.280
share similar words, for example tree in French
is arbre, tree in Italian is albero and tree

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.660
in Spanish is árbol.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:50.200
Whereas countries far away from each other
have virtually zero similarities in their

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.200
languages.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.500
This is why the English alphabet looks so
different to the Japanese alphabet, because,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.380
both languages were developed in complete
isolation from each other.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:06.010
However in the modern day where everyone is
so well connected and we can communicate with

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:11.100
people on the other side of the world in seconds,
wouldn’t it be beneficial if we all spoke

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.930
the same language.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:17.710
Many predict that a single global language
would significantly increase collaboration

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:24.770
and productivity, and some groups have actually
pushed for a universal language, but all attempts

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.400
to do so have so far failed.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.010
The problem is, that if the world were to
adopt a universal language, who would have

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.791
to give up their native tongue and who gets
to keep it, it’s not an easy decision.

00:05:37.791 --> 00:05:44.570
Language is just too deeply embedded within
our cultures to just give it up.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:48.610
To do so would be surrendering your countries
culture and history.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:54.810
So, for the foreseeable future, languages
as we know them today are here to stay.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:06:00.570
But language does change, and quite rapidly
in fact.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:05.919
In the short time we’ve had the internet
the English language has already changed quite

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:07.350
considerably.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:14.710
In March 2011 the Oxford English Dictionary
officially recognised “LOL” as a word.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:19.750
But what will the English language look like
in 1000 years from now.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:25.230
Well Justin B Rye challenged this question,
taking all the effects of different cultures

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.990
and technology into consideration.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:32.740
He proposed a new language, Futurese.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:38.510
Justin took the following sentence “We children
beg you, teacher, that you should teach us

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:44.889
to speak correctly, because we are ignorant
and we speak corruptly” and translated it

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.870
into Futurese to illustrate what it might
look like in 1000 years.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.770
He came up with this:

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:59.600
When pronounced correctly it reads:
zaw khee-ud we-shong yuh teez-og duh yagaw-red-uket’n

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:06.720
wuh-tsawg’n leedla khuz bn eugnurr’n n
wuh-tsawg khurup

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.570
Sounds like something from science fiction
doesn’t it?

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:15.320
But you can just imagine people speaking something
similar to this in the year 3000.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:21.960
And finally I’ll leave you with these facts:
There is a language in Botswana that consists

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.389
of mainly 5 types of clicks.

00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:33.300
Most languages have over 50,000 words or more,
but most speakers only know and use a fraction

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.300
of them.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:40.360
In fact, in everyday conversation people only
use the same few hundred words.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:46.380
India is home to a menagerie of different
languages, 780 different languages are spoken

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:56.150
in India, however 220 of them were lost in
the last 50 years.

